// draws it over the screen through an offset map (the only draws with indirect texturing in the game). "half" makes that copy half the size per side
// (a quarter of the pixels); the composite is sampled by normalised coordinates, so it follows, and it is the final picture there, so it gets softer.
// The copy's texture is learned from the composite (its first colour texture), so the first frame of the effect is still copied at full size.
bool g_warbleHalf = false;
uint32_t g_warbleTex = 0;
// MSC_STADIUM_HEAVY (np/scenecut.h): set by the game's VgxHeavyStadium from the load of a stadium named there to its end. While it is on: warble at half size, the effects pass
// in every scene class at a quarter of each side from 325000 pixels (Effects Quality FASTEST, open play included), and a floor of 0.70 per side at 30 and at 60. All of it is read
// frame by frame on the back end's thread; a change falls in a load, when none of these is on the screen.
std::atomic<int> g_heavyStadium{0};
inline bool HeavyStadium() { return g_heavyStadium.load(std::memory_order_relaxed) != 0; }
// 1.3 MSC_FX_COMPOSITE=0|1 (default 0): the effects compositor. Every scene sends its large additive and soft-alpha particle draws to the one low-resolution accumulation target
// (half or quarter side, chosen per buffer from the fill: vgx_fxcomp.h), by the pixels of the run alone (MSC_FX_HEAVY_PX's rule, in every scene), and composites it once. The older
// switches (MSC_FX_LOWRES*, MSC_FX_HEAVY_PX, MSC_FX_GOV, MSC_STADIUM_HEAVY, MSC_FX_LOWRES_MAXH) keep their meaning; set explicitly they override what the compositor picks.
inline bool FxCompositeOn() {
    const bool on = static_cast<bool>(VGX_KNOB(EnvInt("MSC_FX_COMPOSITE", 0) != 0));
    return on;
}
// 1.3 MSC_MEGA_SHARP=0|1 (default 0; needs MSC_FX_COMPOSITE=1): in Mega Strike scenes (the minigame and, by the game's hint VgxMegaSharp, the captain's launch and the result) the ball and the
// things round it stay sharp. The effects compositor draws sprites into a reduced target (a third of the picture high with MSC_FX_LOWRES_MAXH=224, a half otherwise) tested against a reduced copy
// of the scene's depth and composites it over the picture: a whole reduced texel is lit or hidden, so the halo of the ball overwrote its edge in 3 pixel steps. With the switch on, in a Mega
// scene: (1) the target is not capped by MSC_FX_LOWRES_MAXH (a half, not a third), (2) the depth copy takes the NEAREST of the four depths of a texel (MSC_FX_COMPOSITE_EDGE=2: the ball's edge
// texels hide the sprites behind it, so the picture's own full-size edge stays whole). Render only: the game reads nothing back. g_megaSharpBuf: this buffer is a Mega Strike's with the switch on.
bool g_megaSharpBuf = false;
bool g_megaSharpEdge = false;   // level 1 in the captain's launch / result cut-scenes (the game's cut hint, VgxMegaCutHint): only the nearest-depth copy; the scene is not a Mega scene (its size and policy are the cut-scene's)
std::atomic<int> g_megaCutHint{0};
std::atomic<uint32_t> g_megaSharpBufs{0}, g_megaSharpRuns{0};   // (action counters: buffers drawn with the rules, effect runs that used them)
// MSC_MEGA_SHARP: 0 off, 1 the rules in the Mega minigame only, 2 also in the captain's launch and result cut-scenes (the game's hint VgxMegaSharp: they then count as Mega scenes: held size, one sample, no scene policy).
inline int MegaSharpLevel() {
    const int v = static_cast<int>(VGX_KNOB(static_cast<int>(EnvInt("MSC_MEGA_SHARP", 0))));
    return FxCompositeOn() ? (v < 0 ? 0 : (v > 2 ? 2 : v)) : 0;
}
inline bool MegaSharpOn() { return MegaSharpLevel() >= 1; }
// 1.3 MSC_MEGA_BALLS=0|1 (code default 0, the Vita plan 1; needs MSC_MEGA_SHARP >= 1): the balls that fly in during a Mega Strike (the Mega Save, and the shots of the captain's
// launch cut-scene) keep a clean outline at the picture's own size. Their lightning and halo sprites (7-13 million pixels a picture at the game's size) stay in the effects target
// (a quarter of the picture per side when the fill is that high), and with MSC_MEGA_SHARP alone the nearest-depth copy hid them over every reduced texel that touches a ball:
// a dark ring in 4-pixel steps round each ball, which read as a pixelated ball. With the switch on, in the buffers MSC_MEGA_SHARP acts in: (1) the depth copy reads the picture's
// depth at the texel centre, (2) the composite does nearest-depth upsampling: it reads the picture's own depth at the pixel and at the four texel centres round it; where they all
// agree (MSC_MEGA_BALLS_EPS millionths, default 2000) it keeps the filtered light, at an edge it takes the light of the texel whose depth is nearest the pixel's. So the outline of
// a ball (a hand, any nearer surface) is the picture's full-size one, the glow reaches it, and light in front of the ball stays on it. The sprites' fill is unchanged; the composite
// reads 5 depth and 2 colour texels a pixel against 1. Render only: the game reads nothing back. g_megaBallsRun: this run uses the rules.
bool g_megaBallsRun = false;
uint64_t g_megaBallsFrame = 0;   // (the buffer FxDecide last saw: the diagnostic line)
std::atomic<uint32_t> g_megaBallsRuns{0}, g_megaBallsComp{0};
double g_megaBallsPx = 0.0;   // (the composites' picture pixels with the rule: the cost line)   // (action counters: runs with the farthest-depth copy, composites with the depth-edge rule)
inline bool MegaBallsOn() {
    const bool on = static_cast<bool>(VGX_KNOB(EnvInt("MSC_MEGA_BALLS", 0) != 0));
    return on && MegaSharpOn();
}
